<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Judas of Galilee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 6 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Judas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> was a Jewish leader who led resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-sulpicius-quirinius">Quirinius</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> became a Roman province in 6 CE, teaching that paying tax to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> meant accepting a human master in place of God. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> calls his movement a 'fourth philosophy' beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essenes">Essenes</a> and links it to later rebels against Rome.</p></div>
